  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Wednesday...\n\nDynamic upper trof moving slowly thru western MT, southeast ID and\nWY will bring widespread precipitation through this evening,\nincluding heavy mountain snow on north aspects above 7kft. All of\nthis is covered well by the current forecast including winter \nhighlights for the Beartooth-Absaroka, Pryor & Bighorn Mountains.\nExpect precip to taper off in the west by midday (scattered light \nshowers still possible on the back side of the trof under the \ncool air aloft), central parts by evening, and the east sometime\nlate tonight or early Friday. Greatest precip amounts will be in\nour southern upslope areas, 0.75-1.00\", with a 30% chance of\nexceeding an inch along the immediate north aspects. Regarding\nsnowfall, look for 8-15\" in the Beartooth-Absarokas and 5-10\" over\nthe Pryors and Bighorns. Snow will impact conditions over the\nBeartooth Highway (scheduled to open Friday) as well as US-14\nthrough the Bighorns.\n\nA few smaller details yet to be worked out. There will be a region\nof mid level frontogenesis propagating west-to-east that should \nbe a focus of heaviest precip as it passes. As a result, look for \nan area of enhanced precip extending NE of our southern upslope \nareas, with ensembles suggesting in a general sense this will \noccur from near Pryor to Forsyth-ish. Rainfall amounts in excess \nof a half inch are likely to occur where this frontogenesis \nlingers longest. Something to watch. Next, while burn scars will \nneed to be watched, rainfall rates (0.10-0.20\"/hr) are not \nexpected to be great enough to cause runoff problems. Also, snow \nlevels (about 7kft at 05z per obs and mesoanalysis) are expected \nto fall to near 6kft by 12z. This puts snowfall above Red Lodge \nand Story but not by much. It is possible that thru additional \ndynamic cooling we see a brief period of wet snow make it down to\nnear these locations...but even minor accumulation is not likely.\nFinally, with the surface trof lingering near the Dakotas border \nat midday and moisture return to its east, there is a risk of \nt-storms in our far east in the afternoon, ahead of the main area \nof precip. With capes of perhaps several hundred j/kg, 0-6km bulk \nshear up to 25kts, and pwats of 0.75\", storms would seem to have a\nchance of producing up to half inch hail and localized downpours.\nThat said, there is overwhelming model consensus for the greater \ninstability to be in the western Dakotas.\n\nDescent spreads in from west to east behind the trof late today \nand tonight, with perhaps some light showers lingering in our far \neast into Friday morning. Tomorrow will be drier & warmer with \ntemps back to the 60s (after mid 40s to near 60F today). A weak \nshortwave within the increasingly zonal flow may be enough to \nproduce weak diurnal convection in the afternoon and early \nevening. Warming trend continues this weekend under a building \nridge. Look for 70s Saturday and 80s Sunday. Again, these will be \nmostly dry days but cannot rule out some weak diurnal showers\nespecially close to the mountains.\n\nFlow backs to the SW Monday ahead of the next Pacific trof, and we\ncould see temps near 90F on Memorial Day. Thereafter things get \nsketchier. Cluster analyses continue show great spread in \nsolutions regarding the trof's track through the western CONUS, \nboth in terms of timing and location. For now, look for gradual \ncooling and increasing chances of showers/t-storms Tuesday & \nWednesday. We should see some degree of moisture return up the \nhigh plains ahead of the low, and if things come together we could\nsee a risk of severe t-storms at some point. It is also possible \nthat we see another precip event if the low takes a favorable \ntrack. All this is of low confidence at this time.\n\nJKL",
